        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 784,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 706,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the inverse error function to every element of a tensor: given erf(x), it returns x.\n\nIt turns evenly spread random numbers into normally distributed ones - 1.414 times erfinv(2u - 1), for u between 0 and 1 - and in general turns a probability back into a distance in standard deviations.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nDefined only between -1 and 1. An S lying on its side: nearly straight through the middle (erfinv(0.5) = 0.477), then shooting up near 1 and down near -1. erfinv(0.99) = 1.82, erfinv(0.9999) = 2.75.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.erfinv\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n1 and -1 give plus and minus infinity; anything outside -1 to 1 gives NaN (not a number). That is why the demo runs from -0.99 to 0.99.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-0.99 to 0.99 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is erfinv of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -0.99 to 0.99, drawn from -2 (bottom) to 2 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (inside -1 to 1).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.erf is the function this undoes. t.special.ndtri is the same idea for the normal distribution, scaled so that a probability 0 to 1 gives a value in standard deviations.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 690,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the inverse error function to every element of a tensor: given erf(x), it returns x.\n\nIt turns evenly spread random numbers into normally distributed ones - 1.414 times erfinv(2u - 1), for u between 0 and 1 - and in general turns a probability back into a distance in standard deviations.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nDefined only between -1 and 1. An S lying on its side: nearly straight through the middle (erfinv(0.5) = 0.477), then shooting up near 1 and down near -1. erfinv(0.99) = 1.82, erfinv(0.9999) = 2.75.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.erfinv\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\n1 and -1 give plus and minus infinity; anything outside -1 to 1 gives NaN (not a number). That is why the demo runs from -0.99 to 0.99.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-0.99 to 0.99 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is erfinv of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -0.99 to 0.99, drawn from -2 (bottom) to 2 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (inside -1 to 1).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.erf is the function this undoes. t.special.ndtri is the same idea for the normal distribution, scaled so that a probability 0 to 1 gives a value in standard deviations."
        },
